//? Title: Reading an Iris Decision Tree: Splits, Leaf Shares and Held-Out Checks
//? Subtitle: A tiny exact split, the original nine-node CART fit, and what training accuracy leaves unanswered
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given four flower measurements, we want a short sequence of threshold
questions that predicts one of three Iris species. Greedy recursive
partitioning makes those questions readable, but a perfect fit to the
observed flowers does not establish accuracy on new flowers. We begin
with six hand-checkable observations, retain the original depth-three
Iris fit and predictions, inspect the actual fitted nodes, and compare
training diagnostics with a reproducible held-out split. Native C++
diagrams, plots and an annotated confusion matrix are generated by the
same complete programs that compute the tables.
)

\toc

> One threshold that can be checked by hand

Six observations have x=1,2,3 in class A and x=4,5,6 in class B.
The root proportions are one half each, so its Gini impurity is
1−(1/2)²−(1/2)²=1/2. Candidate thresholds are the midpoints
1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5 and 5.5. Their weighted Gini decreases are
0.10, 0.25, 0.50, 0.25 and 0.10. The middle threshold makes both
children pure and is the unique best split. Root depth is zero;
max_depth=1 permits this one question.

The diagram below comes from the fitted model's actual stored nodes.
Left means x<=3.5, right means x>3.5. It is not a separately
constructed illustration of what we think the model did.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dt_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data;
  data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{1,2,3,4,5,6});
  data.add_column("label",std::vector<std::string>{"A","A","A","B","B","B"});
  const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier model(data,"label ~ x",{.max_depth=1});
  std::print("Tiny fit: {} observations; {} nodes; {} leaves; depth {}; accuracy {:.2f}%.\n\n",
    model.observations(),model.node_count(),model.leaf_count(),model.depth(),100*model.training_accuracy());
  std::print("{}\n",model.node_frame().to_md());
  model.plot_tree().save_svg("build/dt_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Tiny fit: 6 observations; 3 nodes; 2 leaves; depth 1; accuracy 100.00%.

| node | depth | feature | threshold | left | right | n | impurity | class |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 0 | 0 | x | 3.5 | 1 | 2 | 6 | 0.5 | A |
| 1 | 1 |  | NA | -1 | -1 | 3 | 0.0 | A |
| 2 | 1 |  | NA | -1 | -1 | 3 | 0.0 | B |

\image(figures/dt_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The actual fitted root asks x<=3.5. Its six rows split into two pure three-row leaves, reducing Gini impurity from one half to zero.)
\alttext(A native fitted-tree diagram shows a root with threshold 3.5 and two pure leaves predicting A and B.)

> What this CART implementation optimizes

At a node with n rows and class fractions p_k, the two supported criteria are

\[
G=1-\sum_kp_k^2,\qquad H=-\sum_{k:p_k>0}p_k\log_2 p_k.
\]

Gini is the default. A threshold divides the rows into left and right
sets. Its local benefit is

\[
\Delta=I_{parent}-{n_L\over n}I_L-{n_R\over n}I_R.
\]

The learner sorts rows separately for each candidate predictor at each
node, sweeps class counts across consecutive distinct values, and tries
their separating midpoints. A node with n rows and p tried predictors
therefore costs O(p n log n) for sorting plus the class-count impurity
work. Accepted gains must improve the current best by more than 10⁻¹²;
ties within that tolerance keep the earlier predictor/threshold.
Ordinary CART tries all design columns. A nonzero max_features requests
a seeded random subset per node for ensemble use.

Growth stops at pure nodes, the depth limit, too few rows to split,
or no admissible gain exceeding min_impurity_decrease. Every candidate
child must also satisfy min_samples_leaf. This implementation uses
local node impurity decrease for that option; it does not multiply
the stopping threshold by the node's fraction of the whole dataset.
It provides a greedy unpruned CART-style tree, not exhaustive search
over tree structures or cost-complexity pruning.

For importance, each chosen predictor receives (n_node/n_total)Δ.
Normalize the resulting nonnegative credits to sum to one when their
sum is positive; an unsplit model instead has all-zero importance.
This measures use by this fitted tree. It does not establish that a
zero-importance measurement is irrelevant to every other model or population.

Prediction follows stored <= thresholds to a leaf. The majority class
wins, with class ties resolved in sorted class order. predict_detail
returns empirical training class shares in that leaf, not a separately
calibrated probability for an unseen population. Missing training rows
are dropped by the formula design; missing prediction rows receive an
empty label/probability vector, and unseen categorical levels throw.

> Retain the original depth-three Iris fit

The canonical embedded dm::datasets::iris() frame supplies 150 rows,
four numeric measurements in centimetres and one species column.
There are 50 rows per species. No file download or companion checkout
is needed. We fit the original four-predictor formula and depth three.
The node table and the diagram are two views of the same fitted model.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dt_iris_tree.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier model(iris,
    "Species ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Length + Petal.Width",{.max_depth=3});
  std::print("Iris frame: {} rows, {} columns.\n\n",iris.nrows(),iris.ncols());
  std::print("Tree: {} nodes, {} leaves, depth {}. Training accuracy {:.2f}%.\n\n{}\n",model.node_count(),model.leaf_count(),model.depth(),100*model.training_accuracy(),model.node_frame().to_md());
  if(model.node_count()!=9 || model.leaf_count()!=5 || std::abs(model.training_accuracy()-146.0/150)>1e-12)
    throw std::runtime_error("original fitted structure changed");
  model.plot_tree().save_svg("build/dt_iris_tree.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Iris frame: 150 rows, 5 columns.

Tree: 9 nodes, 5 leaves, depth 3. Training accuracy 97.33%.

| node | depth | feature | threshold | left | right | n | impurity | class |
| ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| 0 | 0 | Petal.Length | 2.45 | 1 | 2 | 150 | 0.667 | setosa |
| 1 | 1 |  | NA | -1 | -1 | 50 | 0.000 | setosa |
| 2 | 1 | Petal.Width | 1.75 | 3 | 6 | 100 | 0.500 | versicolor |
| 3 | 2 | Petal.Length | 4.95 | 4 | 5 | 54 | 0.168 | versicolor |
| 4 | 3 |  | NA | -1 | -1 | 48 | 0.041 | versicolor |
| 5 | 3 |  | NA | -1 | -1 | 6 | 0.444 | virginica |
| 6 | 2 | Petal.Length | 4.85 | 7 | 8 | 46 | 0.043 | virginica |
| 7 | 3 |  | NA | -1 | -1 | 3 | 0.444 | virginica |
| 8 | 3 |  | NA | -1 | -1 | 43 | 0.000 | virginica |

\image(figures/dt_iris_tree.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original nine-node, five-leaf tree is retained. It first separates setosa at Petal.Length<=2.45, then uses petal width and length; four training rows are misclassified.)
\alttext(A native diagram shows all nine actual fitted Iris nodes, their thresholds, sample counts, impurities and five class-coloured leaves.)

The root's Gini impurity is 2/3. Its first split separates 50 pure
setosa rows from 100 rows equally divided between the other species,
so its gain is 2/3−(50/150)0−(100/150)(1/2)=1/3.
Petal.Width offers an equally separating root question; deterministic
predictor order keeps Petal.Length. Tree topology and importance can
therefore differ between implementations with equally good tied splits.

> Training errors and impurity importance

The plotted points use true species colours; a black overlaid marker
identifies each training mistake. The three-by-three table uses actual
species as rows and predictions as columns, in the printed class order.
The petal view is a projection of the four-predictor model, and the
importance panel reports the full fit's normalized impurity credits.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dt_iris_diagnostics.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier model(iris,"Species ~ .",{.max_depth=3});
  std::print("Class order:");for(const auto& label:model.classes())std::print(" {}",label);std::print(".\n\n");
  std::print("| actual | predicted setosa | predicted versicolor | predicted virginica |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const auto cm=model.confusion_matrix();
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<3;++i)std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",model.classes()[i],cm(i,0),cm(i,1),cm(i,2));
  const auto importance=model.feature_importance();
  std::print("\n| predictor | normalized impurity importance |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t j=0;j<importance.size();++j)std::print("| {} | {:.4f} |\n",model.predictor_names()[j],importance[j]);
  auto scatter=model.plot_classification(iris,"Petal.Length","Petal.Width");
  scatter.title("Training rows: black marks are errors").x_label("petal length (cm)").y_label("petal width (cm)").y_limits(0,3.4);
  auto bars=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  bars.bars({0,1,2,3},importance,"",{37,99,235}).x_ticks({0,1,2,3},{"Sepal L","Sepal W","Petal L","Petal W"})
   .x_label("predictor").y_limits(0,.75).y_label("normalized impurity decrease").title("This fit uses only petal measurements");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(scatter).add(bars).size(1100,550)
   .title("Original four-predictor fit: 146 of 150 training rows correct").fit_panels()
   .save_svg("build/dt_iris_diagnostics.svg");
}
```
