> A separate held-out check

Keep depth three fixed and split the original rows with
train_test_split(iris,0.2,42,"Species"). Stratification holds out
ten rows from each species, leaving 120 training rows and 30 test rows.
The next model is fitted only to those 120 training rows. Its test
predictions build the annotated ConfusionMatrix through actual/predicted
label pairs. The full-data fit above is an interpretation of all 150
observations; it is not the model evaluated on these held-out rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dt_iris_holdout.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),.2,42,"Species");
  const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier model(train,"Species ~ .",{.max_depth=3});
  const auto predicted=model.predict(test);dm::nn::ConfusionMatrix cm(model.classes().size());
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<test.nrows();++i) {
    const auto actual=test.string_at("Species",i);
    const auto a=std::find(model.classes().begin(),model.classes().end(),actual)-model.classes().begin();
    const auto b=std::find(model.classes().begin(),model.classes().end(),predicted[i])-model.classes().begin();
    cm.add(a,b);
  }
  std::print("Split: {} training rows; {} held-out rows. Depth-three training accuracy {:.2f}%; held-out accuracy {:.2f}%.\n\n",
    train.nrows(),test.nrows(),100*model.training_accuracy(),100*cm.accuracy());
  std::print("| actual | predicted setosa | predicted versicolor | predicted virginica |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<3;++i)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",model.classes()[i],cm.cells()[i][0],cm.cells()[i][1],cm.cells()[i][2]);
  dm::nn::ConfusionMatrixPlotOptions options;options.labels=model.classes();options.cell_size=120;options.font_size=20;
  options.title="Held-out Iris: 30 rows, depth fixed at three";
  cm.save_svg("build/dt_iris_holdout.svg",options);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Split: 120 training rows; 30 held-out rows. Depth-three training accuracy 98.33%; held-out accuracy 93.33%.

| actual | predicted setosa | predicted versicolor | predicted virginica |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 10 | 0 | 0 |
| versicolor | 0 | 9 | 1 |
| virginica | 0 | 1 | 9 |
\image(figures/dt_iris_holdout.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Counts come from predictions on the 30 held-out rows, using a tree fitted only on the other 120. Rows are true species and columns are predicted species; no normalization is applied.)
\alttext(An annotated three-class confusion matrix shows held-out Iris predictions, with true labels running top to bottom and predictions left to right.)

This depth-three subset fit gets 118 of its 120 training rows correct
(98.33%) and 28 of the 30 held-out rows correct (93.33%). Its two test
errors exchange one versicolor and one virginica.

One test row changes accuracy by 1/30, about 3.33 percentage points.
This small, single split is a limited check on generalization, not a
precise population performance estimate or a ranking against LDA/SVM.
Those methods must be fitted and scored on the same rows before their
errors can be compared fairly. Earlier companion reports' training
percentages alone cannot establish such a comparison.

> Depth controls training fit and tree size

Repeat the original depths 1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10. The full-data fit retains
the original accuracy/node-count curve. Separate 120-row fits reuse the
same split, and we print their training and held-out scores alongside.
Each depth builds a fresh tree. These repeated test views are exploratory;
using them to choose depth would make this test set part of selection,
requiring a new evaluation or nested resampling for an independent claim.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dt_iris_depth.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(iris,.2,42,"Species");
  std::vector<double> depths,full_acc,train_acc,test_acc,full_nodes,train_nodes;
  std::print("| max depth | full-data training % | full nodes | 120-row training % | 30-row test % | 120-row nodes |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t d:{1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10}) {
    const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier full(iris,"Species ~ .",{.max_depth=d});
    const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier model(train,"Species ~ .",{.max_depth=d});
    const auto predicted=model.predict(test);std::size_t correct=0;
    for(std::size_t i=0;i<test.nrows();++i)correct+=predicted[i]==test.string_at("Species",i);
    const double score=100.0*correct/test.nrows();
    std::print("| {} | {:.2f} | {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {} |\n",d,100*full.training_accuracy(),full.node_count(),100*model.training_accuracy(),score,model.node_count());
    depths.push_back(d);full_acc.push_back(100*full.training_accuracy());train_acc.push_back(100*model.training_accuracy());test_acc.push_back(score);
    full_nodes.push_back(full.node_count());train_nodes.push_back(model.node_count());
  }
  auto a=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  a.line(depths,full_acc,"150 fit",{37,99,235}).line(depths,train_acc,"120 fit",{22,163,74})
   .line(depths,test_acc,"30 test",{220,38,38}).y_limits(60,115).x_label("maximum depth").y_label("accuracy (%)").title("Training fit versus one held-out split");
  auto b=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  b.line(depths,full_nodes,"150 fit",{37,99,235}).points(depths,full_nodes,"",{37,99,235},5)
   .line(depths,train_nodes,"120 fit",{22,163,74}).y_limits(0,23).x_label("maximum depth").y_label("nodes, including leaves").title("Size stops growing when splits stop");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,550)
   .title("Depth sensitivity: resubstitution and held-out evidence are different").fit_panels().save_svg("build/dt_iris_depth.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| max depth | full-data training % | full nodes | 120-row training % | 30-row test % | 120-row nodes |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 66.67 | 3 | 66.67 | 66.67 | 3 |
| 2 | 96.00 | 5 | 97.50 | 90.00 | 5 |
| 3 | 97.33 | 9 | 98.33 | 93.33 | 7 |
| 4 | 99.33 | 15 | 99.17 | 93.33 | 11 |
| 5 | 100.00 | 17 | 100.00 | 93.33 | 13 |
| 6 | 100.00 | 17 | 100.00 | 93.33 | 13 |
| 8 | 100.00 | 17 | 100.00 | 93.33 | 13 |
| 10 | 100.00 | 17 | 100.00 | 93.33 | 13 |
\image(figures/dt_iris_depth.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original full-data tree reaches perfect training fit at depth five with 17 nodes. The separate held-out curve uses models fitted to only 120 rows; it is not a promise that deeper trees improve new-data accuracy.)
\alttext(Two panels compare full-data training, subset training and held-out accuracy by depth, and the node counts of the two independently fitted training sets.)

On this split, test accuracy is 93.33% from depth three onward, even
while training accuracy reaches 100% and the subset tree grows to 13
nodes. The extra fit to training rows supplies no observed test gain.

> Axis-aligned regions and the original new-flower predictions

Fit a separate depth-three tree with only petal length and width for
this two-dimensional picture. The plot samples its predictions on a
regular grid of pale points, then overlays observed flower measurements.
It visualizes axis-aligned threshold regions; it is not an exact vector
polygon construction or a projection of an arbitrary four-dimensional
boundary. On this dataset the four-predictor fit never uses sepal
features, so the two-predictor fit has the same training score.

The prediction table keeps the original four supplied flowers and the
original four-predictor model. The last flower reaches a three-row leaf
containing one versicolor and two virginica training rows. Its 1/3 and
2/3 shares describe that leaf's training composition. They are not a
guarantee of a two-thirds chance of a correct future prediction.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dt_iris_regions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier boundary(iris,"Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width",{.max_depth=3});
  auto plot=boundary.plot_decision_regions("Petal.Length","Petal.Width",90);
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{22,163,74}};
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<boundary.classes().size();++k) {
    const auto subset=iris.filter([&](const auto& row){return row.get_string("Species")==boundary.classes()[k];});
    plot.points(subset.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),subset.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values(),"",colors[k],4);
  }
  plot.legend(boundary.classes(),colors).title("Depth-three petal tree: sampled regions and observed flowers")
   .x_label("petal length (cm)").y_label("petal width (cm)").y_limits(-.3,3.3).save_svg("build/dt_iris_regions.svg");
  std::print("Two-predictor training accuracy {:.2f}%.\n\n",100*boundary.training_accuracy());
  const dm::stats::DecisionTreeClassifier model(iris,"Species ~ .",{.max_depth=3});
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame flowers;
  flowers.add_column("Sepal.Length",std::vector<double>{5.1,6,6.5,6.2});
  flowers.add_column("Sepal.Width",std::vector<double>{3.5,2.7,3,2.8});
  flowers.add_column("Petal.Length",std::vector<double>{1.4,4.5,5.5,4.8});
  flowers.add_column("Petal.Width",std::vector<double>{.2,1.5,2,1.8});
  const auto q=model.predict_detail(flowers);
  std::print("| flower row | predicted species | P(setosa) | P(versicolor) | P(virginica) |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<q.class_label.size();++i)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n",i,q.class_label[i],q.probability[i][0],q.probability[i][1],q.probability[i][2]);
}
```
