Two-predictor training accuracy 97.33%.

| flower row | predicted species | P(setosa) | P(versicolor) | P(virginica) |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | setosa | 1.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 |
| 1 | versicolor | 0.0000 | 0.9792 | 0.0208 |
| 2 | virginica | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 1.0000 |
| 3 | virginica | 0.0000 | 0.3333 | 0.6667 |
\image(figures/dt_iris_regions.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The two-predictor tree samples rectangular decision regions, overlaid with the canonical data. The original four-predictor predictions and leaf class shares are recomputed separately.)
\alttext(A petal-length versus petal-width plot shows pale sampled class regions with horizontal and vertical boundaries and observed flowers overlaid in species colours.)

> What the executed study supports

The original depth-three model, four training errors, petal-only
importance, depth saturation and four new-flower predictions are retained.
The small example makes local impurity reduction checkable by hand;
the native tree diagrams expose actual fitted node ids and thresholds.
Perfect training accuracy at greater depth demonstrates capacity to fit
these observed labels. The separate held-out experiment adds evidence
about new rows, with the limits of a small, single exploratory split.

The fitted model is owned and copyable. plot_tree() borrows it without
refitting, returns an independently owned native Diagram and checks its
complete-node cap before layout allocation. The default cap is 255 nodes;
zero disables that optional cap. Node-table and diagram outputs describe
the same stored model, and leaves keep their left-to-right fitted order.

> References

L. Breiman, J. H. Friedman, R. A. Olshen and C. J. Stone (1984),
[Classification and Regression Trees](https://www.routledge.com/Classification-and-Regression-Trees/Breiman-Friedman-Stone-Olshen/p/book/9780412048418/),
introduces the CART framework. The implementation here grows greedy
binary trees with the stopping rules and tie tolerance stated above;
cost-complexity pruning is not included.

The [R datasets Iris documentation](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/iris.html)
describes the standard 150-row frame, centimetre units and three species,
with references to Anderson's collection and Fisher's 1936 analysis.
This report executes the repository's embedded frame directly. Iris
copies can differ in individual recorded values, so the embedded data,
formula, options and deterministic tie rule define these reproducible fits.
